WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.575
Welcome to Day 7: Finance, Invoicing, Billing and QuickBooks.

00:00:06.325 --> 00:00:10.663
We'll start with finance foundations, then move to reconciliation and trust

00:00:10.663 --> 00:00:14.288
accounts, and spend a good block on invoice management.

00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:18.188
After that comes time tracking and QuickBooks, then expenses,

00:00:18.188 --> 00:00:21.038
payments and payroll, and we'll finish with tax,

00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:22.675
records and fraud.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:27.175
By the end of today, you'll be able to support the firm's financial operations

00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:32.562
accurately: reconcile accounts, manage invoices from pre-bill to payment,

00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:37.138
track billable and non-billable time, work confidently in QuickBooks,

00:00:37.138 --> 00:00:40.150
manage the firm's cards and stay audit-ready.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:45.350
Section 1 of 6: Finance Foundations.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.750
We'll look at your role in the firm's finances,

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:51.475
what a real SOP needs, and the financial calendar.

00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:57.462
When you handle an invoice or an expense, you're a real link in the firm's money

00:00:57.462 --> 00:01:00.250
chain, not just filing paperwork.

00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:04.825
A mistake, like two digits swapped, doesn't stay on your desk.

00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:08.775
It flows into the books, the client's bill and the firm's reports.

00:01:09.575 --> 00:01:12.712
First, check the numbers before you pass anything on.

00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:17.450
Then, track financial tasks with dates, like any other recurring work.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.812
Finally, if something looks off, flag it.

00:01:21.812 --> 00:01:24.100
Don't assume someone later will catch it.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.113
Picture this.

00:01:26.113 --> 00:01:30.625
A court-reporter invoice for the Meridian deposition arrives with the right amount

00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:32.550
but the wrong matter number.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:38.050
If you pass it on as is, where does that error show up next, and who has to fix it?

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.150
A good procedure answers five questions: why, who,

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.612
how, how do we check, and who do we call when it goes wrong.

00:01:46.612 --> 00:01:50.650
Each answer is one part of a real procedure, and they come in order.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.837
First, purpose.

00:01:53.837 --> 00:01:56.712
Next, scope: exactly who it applies to.

00:01:56.712 --> 00:01:59.337
Then, procedure: the actual steps.

00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:03.425
After that, controls: the checks built in to prove it was done right.

00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:07.100
Finally, escalation: when and how to report a problem.

00:02:07.925 --> 00:02:09.237
Now picture this.

00:02:09.237 --> 00:02:14.075
A temp starts Monday and will process vendor invoices while you're at a conference.

00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:18.750
Your written procedure says 'approve and pay as usual.' Could they follow it on day

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.300
one?

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.575
What would you add?

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.087
Quick check.

00:02:23.087 --> 00:02:28.150
Which SOP element answers 'who does this process apply to?' Is it scope,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.575
escalation, controls or purpose?

00:02:30.575 --> 00:02:31.850
Take a second.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.125
The answer is scope.

00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:38.675
Scope defines who the procedure covers; purpose explains why it exists.

00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:44.675
Money deadlines are relentless, so keep them all on one calendar,

00:02:44.675 --> 00:02:46.650
with reminders well ahead.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:52.050
Billing cycles, tax deadlines and month-end closes keep coming round.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.587
If they live in three different places, or in your memory,

00:02:55.587 --> 00:02:57.725
one of them will eventually slip.

00:02:58.525 --> 00:03:03.650
First, set automatic reminders about a week to ten days before each deadline.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.300
Then, look at the calendar every week for what's coming up.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.675
After that, if one type of deadline keeps slipping, give it extra buffer.

00:03:13.675 --> 00:03:18.025
Finally, check each cycle whether a date or requirement has changed.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.325
Here's one to think through.

00:03:20.325 --> 00:03:21.800
It's mid-December.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.325
Month-end close, the trust reconciliation, the January estimated tax payment and the

00:03:27.325 --> 00:03:31.062
year-end contractor forms all fall within six weeks.

00:03:31.062 --> 00:03:33.650
Which one do you start first, and why?

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.250
Three numbers tell you whether the financial side is healthy.

00:03:40.025 --> 00:03:41.387
Start with speed.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:46.225
Every day an invoice sits unsent is money the firm has already earned and isn't

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:47.325
collecting.

00:03:47.325 --> 00:03:50.825
And clients question old charges more than fresh ones.

00:03:51.625 --> 00:03:57.675
First, track how long invoices take to go out, against a 24 to 72 hour target.

00:03:57.675 --> 00:04:03.500
Next, track reconciliation accuracy, aiming for 98 to 100 percent.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.875
Then, set an alert when a client's retainer drops below a quarter.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.925
After that, review these numbers on a set schedule.

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:17.325
Finally, if one keeps missing, look at the process, not just effort.

00:04:18.075 --> 00:04:19.775
Take a second with this one.

00:04:19.775 --> 00:04:23.475
The Harlow retainer dropped below 25 percent two weeks ago,

00:04:23.475 --> 00:04:26.612
but the alert went to an inbox nobody checks.

00:04:26.612 --> 00:04:29.637
The client now owes more than the retainer holds.

00:04:29.637 --> 00:04:32.775
What went wrong in the process, not just the person?

00:04:34.675 --> 00:04:35.400
Quick check.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.325
What's the target range for invoice turnaround time?

00:04:38.325 --> 00:04:44.325
Is it no target, whenever it gets done… one to two weeks… one to two hours… or 24 to

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:45.687
72 hours?

00:04:45.687 --> 00:04:46.950
Take a second.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.588
It's 24 to 72 hours, from service completion to invoice sent.

00:04:52.588 --> 00:04:54.550
That's the benchmark target.

00:04:56.475 --> 00:05:00.675
The golden rule of bookkeeping: never approve your own transaction.

00:05:01.475 --> 00:05:06.800
First, label each transaction as income, expense or receivable when you record it.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.325
Next, keep a clear trail: who entered it, when and from what document.

00:05:11.325 --> 00:05:14.837
Then, separate duties, even when it would save time.

00:05:14.837 --> 00:05:17.950
That separation is what makes the books trustworthy.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.375
After that, reconcile regularly, while mistakes are still easy to trace.

00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:28.725
Finally, flag anything that looks like a compliance gap straight away.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.825
Think about this one.

00:05:30.825 --> 00:05:36.500
The usual approver is out, and you're asked to both enter and approve a $2,400

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:37.800
vendor payment.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.900
What do you do, and what do you suggest for next time?

00:05:42.825 --> 00:05:46.775
You don't need to be an accountant, but you do need to read the three reports that

00:05:46.775 --> 00:05:48.975
tell a business's money story.

00:05:49.725 --> 00:05:54.950
The profit and loss, or P&L, shows income minus expenses over a period,

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.088
like a month.

00:05:56.088 --> 00:06:00.012
The balance sheet shows what the business owns and owes on one date:

00:06:00.012 --> 00:06:02.775
assets equal liabilities plus equity.

00:06:02.775 --> 00:06:07.025
The cash flow statement shows the cash that actually came in and went out.

00:06:07.025 --> 00:06:10.425
And client money in trust is never the firm's income.

00:06:12.375 --> 00:06:17.425
First, we compare each month's P&L with last month and the same month last year.

00:06:17.425 --> 00:06:21.987
Next, we check receivables, what clients owe, and how old it is.

00:06:21.987 --> 00:06:25.700
Then we watch recurring expenses for anything new or higher.

00:06:27.625 --> 00:06:32.175
After that, we take questions to the accountant with the exact line and amount.

00:06:32.175 --> 00:06:36.650
Finally, we keep the monthly reports together, named the same way.

00:06:37.425 --> 00:06:38.675
Try this one.

00:06:38.675 --> 00:06:43.675
The P&L shows a healthy profit last quarter, but Elias says there isn't enough cash

00:06:43.675 --> 00:06:45.462
for next month's payroll.

00:06:45.462 --> 00:06:48.875
How can both be true, and which report would you pull?

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.925
Section 2 of 6: Reconciliation and Trust Accounts.

00:06:54.925 --> 00:06:57.837
We'll cover statement of account reconciliation,

00:06:57.837 --> 00:07:02.100
spotting discrepancies, and the core rules for client trust accounts.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.187
Reconciling an account comes down to one formula:

00:07:07.187 --> 00:07:10.562
opening balance, plus invoices, minus payments,

00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:14.275
plus or minus adjustments, equals closing balance.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.812
A statement of account shows what a client owes over time.

00:07:18.812 --> 00:07:22.950
Reconciling it just means checking that formula works out exactly.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.600
First, always work through it in that order.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.250
Next, if income doesn't match deposits, compare the bank statement with the income

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.638
records first.

00:07:33.638 --> 00:07:36.325
Then, don't rebuild every report.

00:07:36.325 --> 00:07:39.350
Start from the numbers that don't match and work back.

00:07:41.275 --> 00:07:44.588
After that, make sure the closing balance matches exactly.

00:07:44.588 --> 00:07:46.575
Don't round away a small gap.

00:07:46.575 --> 00:07:49.775
Finally, write down what caused any adjustment.

00:07:50.525 --> 00:07:52.962
Pause the video and work this one out.

00:07:52.962 --> 00:08:02.437
Opening balance $3,200, invoices $4,800, payments $6,000 and a $150 late fee.

00:08:02.437 --> 00:08:04.175
What's the closing balance?

00:08:04.175 --> 00:08:07.725
The client says they owe $1,850.

00:08:07.725 --> 00:08:09.175
Where do you look first?

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:16.350
When a reconciliation doesn't balance, compare line by line, not just the totals.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:19.787
Matching totals can hide real problems.

00:08:19.787 --> 00:08:24.400
Two mistakes can cancel each other out, or a payment can land on the wrong client's

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:27.750
account and still leave the total looking perfect.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.575
First, compare the ledger with the bank statement one line at a time.

00:08:33.575 --> 00:08:37.525
Next, check that credits went to the right client or account.

00:08:37.525 --> 00:08:41.450
Then, look for reversed entries that might be hiding an error.

00:08:43.375 --> 00:08:47.162
After that, run through the checklist: every invoice listed,

00:08:47.162 --> 00:08:50.750
every payment recorded and every difference explained.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:54.700
Finally, watch for anything that looks like a duplicate payment.

00:08:55.525 --> 00:08:57.088
Now a scenario.

00:08:57.088 --> 00:09:02.350
A $3,000 payment from Meridian was recorded against a client with a similar name,

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:05.275
who's now been sent a statement showing a credit.

00:09:05.275 --> 00:09:08.425
What do you correct, and who do you tell, in order?

00:09:10.425 --> 00:09:14.688
Money in a client trust account belongs to the client until it's earned;

00:09:14.688 --> 00:09:17.100
it is never the firm's money to borrow.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.775
Client money, like retainers and settlements, sits in a special trust account.

00:09:22.775 --> 00:09:26.950
Mixing it with the firm's own funds, even briefly or by accident,

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:28.700
is called commingling.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.675
It's one of the most serious ethics violations a lawyer can commit,

00:09:32.675 --> 00:09:34.575
and it can end careers.

00:09:36.525 --> 00:09:40.650
First, keep trust money and the firm's operating money in completely separate

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:41.725
accounts.

00:09:41.725 --> 00:09:44.812
Next, only move money out of trust for a specific,

00:09:44.812 --> 00:09:49.525
documented reason, like an approved invoice or a signed disbursement.

00:09:49.525 --> 00:09:52.400
Finally, keep a running ledger for each client,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.775
showing exactly what's held for them.

00:09:55.525 --> 00:09:56.712
Picture this.

00:09:56.712 --> 00:10:01.450
A client's $5,000 settlement check is deposited into trust today.

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:05.650
Elias wants the $400 expert fee paid from it right away,

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:10.700
because 'the money's there.' What has to happen before any money leaves trust?

00:10:12.625 --> 00:10:16.675
For a trust account, three numbers must always match exactly:

00:10:16.675 --> 00:10:21.000
the bank statement, the trust ledger and the total of every client's individual

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.050
balance.

00:10:22.825 --> 00:10:26.412
Check at least monthly, and right after anything unusual.

00:10:26.412 --> 00:10:31.525
A difference is never 'probably fine'; it gets traced to the exact transaction.

00:10:31.525 --> 00:10:35.450
And document every reconciliation, even the clean ones.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:37.775
Take a second with this one.

00:10:37.775 --> 00:10:41.025
Your trust reconciliation is off by $1.

00:10:41.025 --> 00:10:45.625
It's 5:30 p.m. on the last day of the month, and everyone wants to leave.

00:10:45.625 --> 00:10:47.400
What do you do with that dollar?

00:10:49.325 --> 00:10:52.400
Section 3 of 6: Invoice Management.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.350
We'll cover billing and invoicing, pre-bill review,

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.925
and following invoices through to payment.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:05.325
An invoice is simple math, rate times hours plus expenses minus any discount,

00:11:05.325 --> 00:11:07.500
but it has to be exactly right.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.462
And beyond the total, every invoice needs three things:

00:11:11.462 --> 00:11:14.662
how much is due, when it's due and the payment terms.

00:11:14.662 --> 00:11:18.375
Without them, clients hesitate, and payment slows down.

00:11:20.325 --> 00:11:23.975
First, check the rate and hours against the engagement terms.

00:11:23.975 --> 00:11:26.775
Then, recheck the final math yourself.

00:11:26.775 --> 00:11:28.863
Finally, send it promptly.

00:11:28.863 --> 00:11:32.450
A perfect invoice sitting unsent doesn't bring in money.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:39.062
Pause the video and try one: 12.5 hours at $350 an hour,

00:11:39.062 --> 00:11:45.188
$240 in filing fees and a 10 percent courtesy discount on the fees only.

00:11:45.188 --> 00:11:48.475
What's the total, and what else must the invoice say?

00:11:50.425 --> 00:11:53.325
Know the contract's limits before you start billing.

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:57.963
Every client agreement can have its own rules: payment terms,

00:11:57.963 --> 00:12:00.975
late fees and sometimes a cap on hours.

00:12:00.975 --> 00:12:05.325
If you only discover the cap when the final bill goes out, it's too late.

00:12:06.125 --> 00:12:08.788
First, track hours against the cap as you go.

00:12:08.788 --> 00:12:11.550
Then, flag it well before you reach it.

00:12:13.475 --> 00:12:17.725
After that, apply late fees and terms consistently, as written.

00:12:17.725 --> 00:12:21.725
Finally, if the terms are unclear, confirm before you bill.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.163
Here's one to think through.

00:12:24.163 --> 00:12:27.550
Harlow has gone six hours over its 40-hour cap,

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:30.075
and nobody sent the 80 percent notice.

00:12:30.075 --> 00:12:32.375
The invoice is due to go out tomorrow.

00:12:32.375 --> 00:12:35.200
What do you tell Elias, and what are his options?

00:12:37.125 --> 00:12:42.100
An invoice mistake the client finds costs far more trust than one we catch first.

00:12:42.875 --> 00:12:47.300
A pre-bill is the draft invoice the attorney reviews before it goes out.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:51.875
It's our last chance to catch mistakes, and they're nearly always the same few.

00:12:51.875 --> 00:12:56.700
We prepare and check it; the attorney decides what's charged or written off.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:01.800
First, we run the pre-bill at the same point every month.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.875
Next, we check the header: client, billing contact,

00:13:04.875 --> 00:13:07.262
matter, dates and any reference number.

00:13:07.262 --> 00:13:12.213
Then we check every time entry: matter, person, rate and a clear description.

00:13:12.213 --> 00:13:17.250
After that, costs: a receipt for each, no duplicates and markups only if allowed.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:20.625
Finally, the math, the retainer to apply and the terms,

00:13:20.625 --> 00:13:23.225
and we send the attorney our flags at the top.

00:13:23.975 --> 00:13:25.100
Picture this.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.500
The Harlow pre-bill shows 42 hours in June.

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:34.512
One entry is at last year's rate, two say only 'file review,' and a $620 court

00:13:34.512 --> 00:13:36.762
reporter cost appears twice.

00:13:36.762 --> 00:13:40.025
What goes in your note to Elias, and in what order?

00:13:41.975 --> 00:13:44.875
Sending an invoice is only the halfway point.

00:13:44.875 --> 00:13:49.263
It's finished when it's paid, and a firm can do great work and still run short of

00:13:49.263 --> 00:13:52.150
cash if nobody follows invoices through.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:56.038
We keep one invoice register with the number, client,

00:13:56.038 --> 00:13:59.625
matter, dates, amount, status and date paid.

00:13:59.625 --> 00:14:02.975
Our follow-up is polite, predictable and written down.

00:14:02.975 --> 00:14:05.788
And anything bigger than a reminder, like a payment plan,

00:14:05.788 --> 00:14:09.550
a late fee or collections, is the attorney's decision.

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:15.263
First, number invoices in one sequence and never reuse a number.

00:14:15.263 --> 00:14:17.288
A cancelled invoice is voided.

00:14:17.288 --> 00:14:21.200
Next, send each invoice to the right billing contact in the format the client asks

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:21.988
for.

00:14:21.988 --> 00:14:25.150
Then, follow a set reminder schedule: before the due date,

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:28.563
on it, then 7, 14 and 30 days late.

00:14:28.563 --> 00:14:33.825
After that, review the aging report every week and send Elias anything over 60 days.

00:14:33.825 --> 00:14:38.975
Finally, record payments the day they arrive, so nobody gets chased after paying.

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:41.013
Try this one.

00:14:41.013 --> 00:14:51.450
Meridian owes $18,400: $6,000 is 45 days overdue and $12,400 is 95 days overdue.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.950
No one has followed up.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:55.950
What do you send today, and what do you ask Elias?

00:14:57.875 --> 00:15:01.575
For many corporate clients, an invoice that isn't in their portal,

00:15:01.575 --> 00:15:04.275
in their format, simply doesn't exist.

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:10.063
Many corporate clients and insurers want invoices uploaded to an e-billing portal,

00:15:10.063 --> 00:15:15.250
often in a standard file format called LEDES, with task and activity codes on each

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:16.538
time entry.

00:15:16.538 --> 00:15:20.375
The portal checks each invoice against the client's billing rules.

00:15:20.375 --> 00:15:25.800
If it rejects the invoice or cuts lines, the firm doesn't get paid until it's fixed.

00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:30.950
First, for each e-billing client we record the portal,

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:36.375
who holds the login, the format and codes, the deadline and the billing contact.

00:15:36.375 --> 00:15:40.800
Next, we keep their billing guidelines with the matter and note the rules that cause

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:42.163
the most cuts.

00:15:42.163 --> 00:15:46.175
Then we make sure time entries have the right codes before the pre-bill.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:53.750
After that, we upload and check the status: accepted, rejected or adjusted.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:58.675
Finally, we bring any cuts or rejections to the attorney and resubmit or appeal

00:15:58.675 --> 00:16:00.175
before the deadline.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.263
Now a scenario.

00:16:02.263 --> 00:16:07.213
Harlow's insurer rejects the May invoice: 'Task code missing on 7 entries;

00:16:07.213 --> 00:16:12.625
2 entries exceed the approved rate.' The resubmission window closes in 10 days.

00:16:12.625 --> 00:16:15.300
What do you do, and what do you need from Elias?

00:16:17.225 --> 00:16:20.025
Retainers are where billing meets trust accounting,

00:16:20.025 --> 00:16:24.225
and mistakes here are ethics problems, not just accounting ones.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:29.400
A retainer paid up front goes into the client trust account and stays the client's

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.788
money until the firm has earned it and billed for it.

00:16:32.788 --> 00:16:37.350
Paying an invoice from it means moving money from trust to the firm's operating

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:38.350
account.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:42.750
And an 'evergreen' retainer has to be topped back up to an agreed level.

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:49.325
First, when a retainer arrives, we confirm it went into trust and record it on the

00:16:49.325 --> 00:16:50.800
client's ledger.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:55.475
Next, the invoice shows the total, the amount applied from the retainer and the

00:16:55.475 --> 00:16:56.800
balance left.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.950
Then we get the attorney's approval in writing before any transfer.

00:17:02.875 --> 00:17:08.000
After that, we transfer exactly that amount and record it with the invoice number.

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.400
Finally, when the balance drops below the agreed level,

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.900
we send the approved top-up request.

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:17.063
Pause the video and work this one out.

00:17:17.063 --> 00:17:21.525
Meridian's evergreen retainer must stay at $10,000 or more.

00:17:21.525 --> 00:17:27.738
The balance is $11,200, and the June invoice is $6,800.

00:17:27.738 --> 00:17:30.725
What does the invoice show, what transfer happens,

00:17:30.725 --> 00:17:33.750
what's the new balance, and what goes to the client?

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:38.163
If payments aren't matched to the right invoices,

00:17:38.163 --> 00:17:41.125
clients get chased for bills they've already paid.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:46.225
Real payments are messy: one wire can cover three invoices,

00:17:46.225 --> 00:17:50.525
a client can pay half, or a bank fee can make it arrive a little short.

00:17:50.525 --> 00:17:54.575
And at the end of each month, the total of all open invoices should match the

00:17:54.575 --> 00:17:56.750
receivables number in the books.

00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:00.150
If it doesn't, something was applied to the wrong place.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.938
First, we record each payment the day it arrives:

00:18:04.938 --> 00:18:08.475
date, amount, method, who paid and any reference.

00:18:08.475 --> 00:18:11.050
Next, we match it to invoice numbers.

00:18:11.050 --> 00:18:15.100
With no note, we match by exact amount, then ask the client.

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:19.850
Then, part payments leave a balance open, and overpayments become a credit until the

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:21.475
attorney decides.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:28.063
Finally, a short payment caused by bank fees gets the fee recorded separately,

00:18:28.063 --> 00:18:29.900
if that's firm policy.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:31.788
Picture this.

00:18:31.788 --> 00:18:38.700
Harlow Industries sends one wire of $7,425 with the note 'May invoices.' There are

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:45.513
three open May invoices: $2,500, $3,000 and $2,000.

00:18:45.513 --> 00:18:49.550
What happened to the missing $75, how do you apply the wire,

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:51.250
and what do you ask Harlow?

00:18:53.175 --> 00:18:57.375
A billing dispute is a request for an explanation, not an attack.

00:18:58.175 --> 00:19:01.950
Most disputes come from honest confusion, not bad faith.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:07.025
First, pull the actual records behind the charge before you reply.

00:19:07.025 --> 00:19:11.525
Next, acknowledge the dispute quickly, even before it's resolved.

00:19:11.525 --> 00:19:14.925
Finally, explain the answer with the supporting details,

00:19:14.925 --> 00:19:18.050
so the client can see how the charge was worked out.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:20.463
Take a second with this one.

00:19:20.463 --> 00:19:24.438
You check the records and find the disputed charge was billed twice:

00:19:24.438 --> 00:19:26.888
once in May and again in June.

00:19:26.888 --> 00:19:30.775
How do you tell the client, and what else do you check before you reply?

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:36.350
Section 4 of 6: Time Tracking and QuickBooks.

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:40.850
We'll cover real-time time tracking, billable versus non-billable hours,

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.250
and the everyday QuickBooks workflows.

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:49.038
Billable time becomes the client's invoice, so record it now,

00:19:49.038 --> 00:19:52.663
as you work or straight afterwards, not from memory later.

00:19:52.663 --> 00:19:55.700
The longer you wait, the less accurate it gets.

00:19:56.500 --> 00:19:59.750
First, write enough detail to support the invoice line:

00:19:59.750 --> 00:20:01.925
what was done, for which matter.

00:20:01.925 --> 00:20:05.800
Finally, compare logged time with the billing calendar regularly,

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:07.800
before invoices go out.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:10.188
Here's one to think through.

00:20:10.188 --> 00:20:15.700
A client questions a 3.5-hour entry that says 'research.' The associate can't

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:17.788
remember what the research was about.

00:20:17.788 --> 00:20:20.700
How would a better time entry have prevented this?

00:20:22.625 --> 00:20:26.900
Getting billable and non-billable right is the difference between an invoice that

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:29.650
gets paid and one the client cuts.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:34.688
Billable time is work the client can be charged for under their engagement letter:

00:20:34.688 --> 00:20:38.650
drafting, research, calls and court time on their matter.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:42.088
Non-billable is everything else, like internal admin,

00:20:42.088 --> 00:20:46.588
training, business development, or work the firm agreed not to charge.

00:20:46.588 --> 00:20:51.450
We record both, because non-billable time still shows where the hours go.

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:57.400
First, every entry gets a date, matter, time in tenths of an hour,

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:00.100
a clear description and billable or not.

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:04.188
Next, we check the engagement letter and the client's guidelines.

00:21:04.188 --> 00:21:07.213
Scheduling and copying are often non-billable.

00:21:07.213 --> 00:21:10.975
Then we write one task per entry, not one long block.

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:17.300
After that, we prepare a pre-bill, so the attorney can decide any write-downs or

00:21:17.300 --> 00:21:18.500
write-offs.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.125
Finally, approved no-charge work can show on the invoice as 'no charge.'

00:21:23.925 --> 00:21:25.050
Try this one.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:29.388
An associate's entry reads: '3.5, Harlow: call with client,

00:21:29.388 --> 00:21:34.800
research, drafted letter, scheduled meeting, copied exhibits.' Harlow's guidelines

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.438
reject block billing and clerical time.

00:21:37.438 --> 00:21:39.875
How do you help fix it before the pre-bill?

00:21:41.825 --> 00:21:46.000
Four everyday tasks cover most of what you'll ever do in QuickBooks.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.300
You don't need to master the whole program.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:51.525
Learn these four until you can do them without thinking.

00:21:53.475 --> 00:21:57.263
First, create an invoice: New, then Invoice, pick the client,

00:21:57.263 --> 00:21:59.788
add the lines with description, rate and hours,

00:21:59.788 --> 00:22:01.675
check the total and send.

00:22:01.675 --> 00:22:06.275
Next, record an expense: New, then Expense, pick who was paid and from which

00:22:06.275 --> 00:22:09.500
account, choose the category and attach the receipt.

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:13.513
Then, reconcile an account: pick the account and statement date,

00:22:13.513 --> 00:22:17.538
tick every transaction that matches the bank and finish only when the difference is

00:22:17.538 --> 00:22:18.775
zero.

00:22:18.775 --> 00:22:24.200
Finally, run the overdue-invoices report, and look first at anything over 60 and 90

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:25.113
days.

00:22:25.113 --> 00:22:27.075
Those need a follow-up call.

00:22:27.825 --> 00:22:32.313
If you have access, pause the video and try the invoice and the reconciliation

00:22:32.313 --> 00:22:33.350
yourself.

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:39.600
QuickBooks can make sure every billable hour and cost reaches the invoice,

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.100
but only if it's recorded the right way.

00:22:42.875 --> 00:22:47.525
In QuickBooks Online, when we record time or a cost and tick 'Billable' for a

00:22:47.525 --> 00:22:49.200
customer, it waits.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:52.800
On their next invoice, QuickBooks shows everything that's waiting,

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.538
and we choose what to add.

00:22:54.538 --> 00:22:57.925
So the invoice is only as good as the entries behind it.

00:22:59.875 --> 00:23:03.988
First, we set up each client as a customer, and each matter under it as a

00:23:03.988 --> 00:23:06.075
sub-customer or project.

00:23:06.075 --> 00:23:10.438
Next, we record time with the person, matter, service item and rate,

00:23:10.438 --> 00:23:13.425
hours, a description, and Billable ticked.

00:23:13.425 --> 00:23:19.025
Then we record client costs, like filing fees, as billable expenses to the matter.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:24.388
After that, we add the waiting time and costs to the invoice,

00:23:24.388 --> 00:23:27.300
checking dates, descriptions and rates.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:33.025
Finally, we run the unbilled time and expenses report to catch anything left behind.

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:35.325
Now a scenario.

00:23:35.325 --> 00:23:40.900
At month-end, that report shows 6.2 hours for Harlow with no matter selected,

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:46.275
and a $435 court reporter bill for Meridian that wasn't marked billable.

00:23:46.275 --> 00:23:50.175
What do you fix, and in what order, before invoices go out?

00:23:52.075 --> 00:23:56.288
The most common QuickBooks mistake is putting an expense in the wrong category,

00:23:56.288 --> 00:23:58.300
so when you're unsure, ask.

00:23:59.075 --> 00:24:03.950
A classic example: recording a cost the client should reimburse as a general office

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:05.088
expense.

00:24:05.088 --> 00:24:09.800
That one mistake breaks both the client's invoice and the firm's own books.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:15.575
First, confirm the right category before you save an expense.

00:24:15.575 --> 00:24:20.775
Next, if a reconciliation isn't exactly zero, look for a missing or duplicated

00:24:20.775 --> 00:24:22.050
transaction.

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:24.700
Don't adjust something unrelated to force it.

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:28.425
Then, check every invoice against the engagement terms.

00:24:28.425 --> 00:24:31.375
The software will happily bill the wrong rate.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:37.388
After that, remember that the online version saves automatically.

00:24:37.388 --> 00:24:40.825
A mistake needs a proper correction, not an undo.

00:24:40.825 --> 00:24:46.100
Finally, review recent entries now and then for repeated category mistakes.

00:24:46.825 --> 00:24:48.550
Take a second with this one.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:52.950
Your bank reconciliation is off by exactly $450,

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:56.900
and you notice a $450 deposit entered twice.

00:24:56.900 --> 00:25:01.125
Where will you look first next time something is off by a round amount?

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:07.263
Bank feeds save hours, but only if someone reviews them properly.

00:25:07.263 --> 00:25:10.625
Otherwise they fill the books with duplicates and guesses.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:15.825
Bank feeds pull transactions from the firm's bank and card accounts into QuickBooks

00:25:15.825 --> 00:25:19.325
automatically, but each one still needs a decision.

00:25:19.325 --> 00:25:23.550
If it's already in the books, like a bill we've already paid, we match it.

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:28.713
If it's new, we add it with a category and payee, and a customer if it's billable.

00:25:28.713 --> 00:25:32.200
Then month-end close is what makes the numbers reliable.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.700
First, review the feed at least weekly.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:40.825
Next, make rules for regular items like rent or subscriptions,

00:25:40.825 --> 00:25:42.350
and check what they did.

00:25:42.350 --> 00:25:45.650
Then, exclude only true duplicates, with a note.

00:25:47.575 --> 00:25:51.813
After that, at month-end, clear the feed, reconcile every account,

00:25:51.813 --> 00:25:55.250
clear uncategorized items and run the reports.

00:25:55.250 --> 00:26:00.050
Finally, once the accountant confirms, set the closing date with a password.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.000
Picture this.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:06.800
The feed shows a $1,200 payment to the firm's credit card company,

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:11.038
and QuickBooks suggests 'Office expenses.' What is it really,

00:26:11.038 --> 00:26:15.125
how should it be recorded and what goes wrong if you accept the suggestion?

00:26:17.075 --> 00:26:21.200
Section 5 of 6: Expenses, Payments and Payroll.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:26.100
We'll cover the firm's credit cards, expense report auditing, and payroll basics.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:31.675
Review a credit card statement before you pay it, not after.

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:34.288
Three related jobs here.

00:26:34.288 --> 00:26:38.538
Paying the cards, where missed payments cost money and damage credit.

00:26:38.538 --> 00:26:42.750
Applying for cards, where we follow the issuer's process precisely.

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:47.575
And tax season, which is really about keeping receipts organized all year long,

00:26:47.575 --> 00:26:49.675
not hunting for them in April.

00:26:51.625 --> 00:26:54.575
First, put card due dates on a real calendar.

00:26:54.575 --> 00:26:58.125
Next, check the statement for anything that shouldn't be there.

00:26:58.125 --> 00:27:02.388
Then, for an application, gather exactly the documents requested,

00:27:02.388 --> 00:27:04.225
no more and no less.

00:27:06.175 --> 00:27:10.775
Finally, test yourself: could you hand over every receipt from the last 90 days

00:27:10.775 --> 00:27:11.775
quickly?

00:27:12.550 --> 00:27:14.288
Here's one to think through.

00:27:14.288 --> 00:27:20.600
Reviewing Elias's card statement, you spot a $129 charge from an unfamiliar merchant

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.450
and a hotel charged twice for the same night.

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:25.600
What do you do before paying the bill?

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:31.775
When a card problem hits, the assistant is usually the first to know,

00:27:31.775 --> 00:27:34.925
and how fast you act decides how much it costs.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:40.788
A billing error, or a charge we don't recognize, can be disputed with the issuer.

00:27:40.788 --> 00:27:45.488
For US credit cards, billing-error disputes should go in writing within 60 days of

00:27:45.488 --> 00:27:47.975
the statement that first showed the charge.

00:27:47.975 --> 00:27:53.288
A lost or stolen card, or suspected fraud, gets reported straight away.

00:27:53.288 --> 00:27:56.525
The issuer usually blocks it and sends a new card.

00:27:58.475 --> 00:28:01.400
First, with an unknown charge, we check receipts,

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:04.875
the merchant's full name and whether someone else used the card.

00:28:04.875 --> 00:28:08.125
Next, for a wrong charge, we go to the merchant first,

00:28:08.125 --> 00:28:10.950
then dispute with the issuer if it isn't fixed.

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:15.763
Then, for fraud or a lost card, we report it using the number on the card or the

00:28:15.763 --> 00:28:18.300
app, never a number from a message.

00:28:20.225 --> 00:28:24.825
After that, when the new card arrives, we update every subscription and automatic

00:28:24.825 --> 00:28:25.800
payment.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:29.700
Finally, we log it all and check the next statement for the credit.

00:28:30.475 --> 00:28:31.663
Try this one.

00:28:31.663 --> 00:28:37.050
Elias texts from an airport: his firm card was declined and he's had a fraud alert.

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:41.950
He needs a hotel tonight, and the same card pays for three software subscriptions.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:43.675
What do you do, in order?

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:49.400
A card statement nobody reconciles hides lost client costs,

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.500
personal charges and fraud.

00:28:52.275 --> 00:28:56.550
Charges for a client matter, like deposition travel or a filing fee,

00:28:56.550 --> 00:29:00.838
are coded to the matter and marked billable so the firm gets the money back.

00:29:00.838 --> 00:29:05.463
Personal charges, even accidental ones, get flagged and repaid,

00:29:05.463 --> 00:29:07.875
never hidden in a business category.

00:29:09.825 --> 00:29:13.650
First, we download the statement and gather that month's receipts.

00:29:13.650 --> 00:29:17.550
Next, line by line, we match each charge to its receipt,

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.913
confirm the purpose and assign the category and matter.

00:29:20.913 --> 00:29:25.138
Then we chase any missing receipts that week, or use the firm's missing-receipt

00:29:25.138 --> 00:29:26.100
form.

00:29:28.025 --> 00:29:32.850
After that, we flag duplicates, unknown charges and personal charges to the right

00:29:32.850 --> 00:29:33.925
person.

00:29:33.925 --> 00:29:38.375
Finally, we reconcile the card in QuickBooks to the statement's ending balance and

00:29:38.375 --> 00:29:40.275
file everything together.

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:42.563
Now a scenario.

00:29:42.563 --> 00:29:47.325
Elias's statement has 34 charges, and you have 29 receipts.

00:29:47.325 --> 00:29:51.250
Two charges are for the Chicago deposition, one looks personal,

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:55.663
a $64 pharmacy charge, and two have no receipt at all.

00:29:55.663 --> 00:29:59.225
How do you finish the reconciliation, and who do you contact?

00:30:01.125 --> 00:30:05.925
Without a receipt, there's no independent proof an expense actually happened.

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:10.900
Checking expense reports isn't about catching fraud after the fact.

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:14.700
It's about making sure each expense is real before it's paid.

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:20.750
First, check each line against its receipt: amount, date and business purpose.

00:30:20.750 --> 00:30:24.950
Next, hold anything missing a receipt or reason until it's explained.

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:28.250
Finally, send every report through the proper approver.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:29.900
Never approve your own.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:31.975
Picture this.

00:30:31.975 --> 00:30:36.663
One staff member bought three laptop accessories from the same shop on the same day,

00:30:36.663 --> 00:30:42.088
at $180, $190 and $175.

00:30:42.088 --> 00:30:45.375
The limit without extra approval is $200.

00:30:45.375 --> 00:30:46.450
What do you do?

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:52.350
Pay vendors according to the terms you agreed, not on reflex.

00:30:53.125 --> 00:30:58.138
Terms like 'net 30' or 'due on receipt' shape the firm's cash flow.

00:30:58.138 --> 00:31:01.325
Paying earlier than needed ties up money with no benefit,

00:31:01.325 --> 00:31:03.688
unless there's an early-payment discount.

00:31:03.688 --> 00:31:08.750
Paying late brings fees, strained relationships and sometimes cut-off service.

00:31:09.550 --> 00:31:12.588
First, confirm each vendor's actual terms.

00:31:12.588 --> 00:31:14.250
Don't assume a default.

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:18.325
Then, line up upcoming payments against expected money coming in,

00:31:18.325 --> 00:31:19.975
so they don't collide.

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.475
Take a second with this one.

00:31:22.475 --> 00:31:27.088
Three large vendor bills are due on the 1st, and the firm's biggest client payment

00:31:27.088 --> 00:31:29.150
is expected on the 15th.

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:32.425
The office manager wants to pay all three immediately.

00:31:32.425 --> 00:31:34.400
What do you flag to Elias?

00:31:36.325 --> 00:31:40.200
Payroll deadlines never move, so your part of it can't be late.

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:44.788
You probably won't run payroll yourself, but you'll touch its edges:

00:31:44.788 --> 00:31:48.500
onboarding paperwork, timesheets and reimbursements.

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:52.975
And salaries and bank details are among the most sensitive data you'll handle.

00:31:53.775 --> 00:31:58.075
First, make sure timesheets and hours are accurate and in before the payroll

00:31:58.075 --> 00:31:59.150
cut-off.

00:31:59.150 --> 00:32:04.250
Next, handle new-hire forms and bank changes as confidential documents.

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:08.275
Finally, put payroll deadlines on the compliance calendar with the other hard

00:32:08.275 --> 00:32:09.450
deadlines.

00:32:10.225 --> 00:32:11.713
Here's one to think through.

00:32:11.713 --> 00:32:14.475
A colleague asks you what the new associate earns,

00:32:14.475 --> 00:32:17.213
'just roughly.' You processed the offer letter.

00:32:17.213 --> 00:32:18.575
How do you answer?

00:32:20.500 --> 00:32:24.225
Section 6 of 6: Tax, Records and Fraud.

00:32:24.225 --> 00:32:29.025
We'll cover tax season support, record retention and fraud red flags in financial

00:32:29.025 --> 00:32:30.200
documents.

00:32:32.125 --> 00:32:36.350
Tax season is easy when you've filed everything properly all year long.

00:32:37.125 --> 00:32:41.775
The real value you bring at tax time isn't a heroic effort in March.

00:32:41.775 --> 00:32:46.613
It's the habit of organizing receipts, invoices and records every week,

00:32:46.613 --> 00:32:50.900
so when the accountant asks for something, you simply find it instead of rebuilding

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.500
a year from memory.

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:57.500
First, become the person who can reliably answer 'do you have this?'

00:32:59.475 --> 00:33:04.800
After that, connect it to reconciliation: good records make reconciling fast.

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:09.950
Finally, build a small daily or weekly filing habit instead of one giant push a

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:10.875
year.

00:33:11.625 --> 00:33:12.825
Try this one.

00:33:12.825 --> 00:33:17.725
The accountant asks for all of last year's business travel receipts by Wednesday.

00:33:17.725 --> 00:33:22.300
Half are filed by date and trip; half are photos in Elias's phone.

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:25.425
What do you do now, and what habit do you start for this year?

00:33:27.325 --> 00:33:32.875
Quarterly estimated taxes are four separate deadlines, each with its own lead time.

00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:35.675
Missing one brings penalties.

00:33:35.675 --> 00:33:40.550
It's the same reminder discipline we use for court deadlines, applied to tax.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:45.213
First, at the start of the year, put all four dates on the financial calendar with

00:33:45.213 --> 00:33:46.850
early reminders.

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:51.013
Next, well before each date, confirm with the accountant that the amount and

00:33:51.013 --> 00:33:52.625
paperwork are ready.

00:33:52.625 --> 00:33:56.375
Finally, keep a record of each payment confirmation.

00:33:57.125 --> 00:33:58.750
Take a second with this one.

00:33:58.750 --> 00:34:02.725
The accountant sends the quarterly amount the day before the deadline.

00:34:02.725 --> 00:34:06.775
It's much higher than last quarter, and Elias is unreachable.

00:34:06.775 --> 00:34:09.850
What do you do, and what do you change for next quarter?

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:16.450
Get the vendor's tax form before you pay them the first time, not at tax time.

00:34:17.200 --> 00:34:20.225
When a business pays contractors above a certain amount,

00:34:20.225 --> 00:34:25.113
it has to file year-end tax forms for them, using the details collected on a form

00:34:25.113 --> 00:34:26.975
called a W-9.

00:34:26.975 --> 00:34:31.325
Chasing a vendor for that in January is stressful and avoidable.

00:34:32.125 --> 00:34:36.463
First, check the vendor list against the W-9 files now and then,

00:34:36.463 --> 00:34:39.100
especially for vendors added mid-year.

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:44.300
Finally, put the year-end filing deadline on the calendar with plenty of lead time.

00:34:45.125 --> 00:34:46.563
Now a scenario.

00:34:46.563 --> 00:34:52.238
In December, you find four contractors paid over the threshold with no W-9 on file.

00:34:52.238 --> 00:34:53.963
Two of them are hard to reach.

00:34:53.963 --> 00:34:57.125
What's your plan, week by week, to January?

00:34:59.025 --> 00:35:03.600
Keeping records isn't enough; you have to be able to find them when someone asks.

00:35:04.375 --> 00:35:07.700
Financial records have minimum periods they must be kept,

00:35:07.700 --> 00:35:10.488
and those vary by type and by state.

00:35:10.488 --> 00:35:14.150
Trust account records often have longer requirements.

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:18.975
And destroying something too early can cause real trouble in an audit or dispute.

00:35:20.925 --> 00:35:24.125
First, know the retention period for each type of record,

00:35:24.125 --> 00:35:26.225
not one rule for everything.

00:35:26.225 --> 00:35:29.875
Next, store records somewhere durable and searchable.

00:35:29.875 --> 00:35:35.225
Finally, build the retention date into the filing, so reviews happen on schedule.

00:35:35.975 --> 00:35:37.063
Picture this.

00:35:37.063 --> 00:35:39.925
A partner wants to clear out the old storage room:

00:35:39.925 --> 00:35:44.838
'Anything more than five years old can go.' The boxes include trust records and

00:35:44.838 --> 00:35:46.725
closed litigation files.

00:35:46.725 --> 00:35:49.500
What do you check before a single box leaves?

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.675
Most fraud doesn't look dramatic; it looks like a small,

00:35:54.675 --> 00:35:57.200
believable detail that's slightly off.

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:01.013
One odd detail is usually just a mistake.

00:36:01.013 --> 00:36:04.275
A pattern of odd details signals real trouble.

00:36:04.275 --> 00:36:09.100
And you see the most detail day to day, so you're often the first line of defense.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:13.525
First, watch for the classic signs: an unfamiliar vendor,

00:36:13.525 --> 00:36:18.850
suspiciously round amounts, duplicate invoice numbers or sudden new bank details.

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:23.363
Next, confirm any change in payment details through a separate channel you already

00:36:23.363 --> 00:36:27.375
trust, never the contact details in the request itself.

00:36:27.375 --> 00:36:32.475
Finally, raise a real concern promptly, rather than trying to solve it alone.

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:34.913
Here's one to think through.

00:36:34.913 --> 00:36:38.475
Last month, you flagged an invoice that turned out to be genuine,

00:36:38.475 --> 00:36:40.275
and you felt embarrassed.

00:36:40.275 --> 00:36:46.688
This month, an email from 'Elias' asks you to buy $1,500 in gift cards for a client,

00:36:46.688 --> 00:36:48.350
quickly and quietly.

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:49.425
What do you do?

00:36:51.375 --> 00:36:53.663
Time to practice what you just learned.

00:36:53.663 --> 00:36:57.113
In today's lab, you'll reconcile a client trust ledger,

00:36:57.113 --> 00:37:01.600
audit a queue of invoices and bills, decide the right follow-up for every open

00:37:01.600 --> 00:37:06.050
invoice, then find the planted errors in a set of billing documents.

00:37:06.050 --> 00:37:07.600
It's your turn now.

